      "text": "To solve this problem, we will use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the following sets:\n\n- Let \\( H \\) be the set of students who received an A in history.\n- Let \\( M \\) be the set of students who received an A in math.\n\nWe are given the following information:\n- The total number of students is \\( |U| = 30 \\).\n- The number of students who received an A in history is \\( |H| = 7 \\).\n- The number of students who received an A in math is \\( |M| = 13 \\).\n- The number of students who received an A in both history and math is \\( |H \\cap M| = 4 \\).\n\nWe need to find the number of students who did not receive an A in either course. First, we use the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students who received an A in at least one of the courses:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = |H| + |M| - |H \\cap M|\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nThis means that 16 students received an A in at least one of the courses. Therefore, the number of students who did not receive an A in either course is:\n\n\\[\n30 - |H \\cup M| = 30 - 16 = 14\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
